<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/nvalt-tasks-rb.7540.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>nvALT now understands GitHub-style task lists. A checkbox is just text in the note, the editor treats it like a task, and the preview draws it as one.</p>

<figure title="" class="animated_vid_frame" data-caption="&#9654; MP4 (651KB)" style="padding-bottom:50.85%" tabindex="0">
              <video class="lazy" muted="" loop="" playsinline="" poster="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/nvalt-task-lists.jpg" style="background:center/contain no-repeat url('https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/nvalt-task-lists.jpg')">
                <source src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/nvalt-task-lists.webm" type="video/webm" />
                <source src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/nvalt-task-lists.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
              </video>
            </figure>

<h3 id="make-a-task">Make a task</h3>

<p>Put the cursor on a line and press <strong>Option-Command-T</strong>. The same command is <strong>Task</strong> in the editor&rsquo;s Format menu.</p>

<p>A plain line turns into a task:</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">-</span> [ ] mail the contract</code></pre></div></div>

<p>If the line is already a list item, the checkbox lands after the marker you already used:</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">*</span> [ ] call mom
<span class="p">1.</span> [ ] book the train</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Press the shortcut again and the checkbox goes away. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">- [ ] mail the contract</code> goes back to <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">- mail the contract</code>.</p>

<h3 id="check-it-off">Check it off</h3>

<p>Click the space between the brackets. A space becomes <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">x</code>, and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">x</code> becomes a space again.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">-</span> [x] mail the contract</code></pre></div></div>

<p>A finished task is crossed out in the editor. The marker and the checkbox stay plain, so you can still see where to click.</p>

<h3 id="keep-going">Keep going</h3>

<p>Return at the end of a task starts the next line as a new open task:</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">-</span> [x] mail the contract
<span class="p">-</span> [ ]</code></pre></div></div>

<p>A numbered task keeps counting, so <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">1. [ ] book the train</code> is followed by <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">2. [ ] </code>.</p>

<p>Return on an empty checkbox clears that line instead of adding another one. Same idea as hitting Return on a bare <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">- </code>.</p>

<h3 id="in-the-preview">In the preview</h3>

<p><a href="https://github.com/apexmarkdown/apex">Apex</a> (which runs the nvALT preview now) already understands GitHub chekboxes. The preview renders these as real checkboxes. A completed item shows up checked and crossed out. Clicking still happens in the editor. The preview is just there to read.</p>

<h3 id="taskpaper-style">TaskPaper-Style</h3>

<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/nvalt-taskpaper-style.jpg"></p>

<p>This isn&rsquo;t new, but nvALT also recognizes <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">@done</code> tags and will strike through any line containing them, so you can use TaskPaper formatting and get a workable task list. There are currently no shortcuts for toggling a task finished. I would have liked to use <span class="keycombo separated" title="Command-D"><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8984;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="key symbol">D</kbd></span> for that, but it&rsquo;s already assigned to &ldquo;Deselect Note&rdquo; and I&rsquo;m not sure how many people already use that instinctively. I&rsquo;ll toy with it.</p>

